#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Classically simulate $\\langle X \\rangle_i$ for each site in the 22-qubit "
"hex-Ising model {#classically-simulate-langle-x-rangle-i-for-each-site-in-"
"the-22-qubit-hex-ising-model}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"First, we use the Qiskit ``Statevector`` class to simulate the exact target "
"values for our experiment. While the 22-qubit problem we're solving is "
"tractable classically, the Qiskit statevector simulator will not scale to "
"demos much larger than this one, and to scale up past 50 qubits or so, we "
"would need to use inexact simulation techniques, such as [Pauli "
"propagation](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/addons/pauli-prop). This 49-qubit "
"experiment allows us to investigate combining error detection with error "
"mitigation at larger scales while maintaining access to the ideal "
"expectation values.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/docs/images/tutorials/probabilistic-"
"error-cancellation-with-logical-noise-models/extracted-"
"outputs/0c82c211-0.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Implement the 49-qubit error-detecting hex-Ising model with a quantum "
"circuit and transpile to QPU backend {#implement-the-49-qubit-error-"
"detecting-hex-ising-model-with-a-quantum-circuit-and-transpile-to-qpu-"
"backend}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"This circuit simulates 4 Trotter steps of a 22-qubit transverse-field Ising "
"Hamiltonian on a hexagonal lattice. The 22 data qubits are embedded in a "
"49-qubit heavy-hex subgraph of the Heron r3 QPU, ``ibm_boston``. The 27 "
"ancilla qubits are used for two purposes: mediating entanglement between the"
" Ising qubits and detecting logical errors during circuit execution. The "
"circuit's entangling layers are implemented such that the mediator qubits "
"return to the ground state $|0\\rangle$ before their terminal measurements. "
"One or more mediator qubits measuring $|1\\rangle$ indicates that the sample"
" was corrupted by a logical error. Discarding these samples can improve the "
"fidelity of the sampled distribution.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"In the graph below, the green qubits represent the 22 Ising qubits, and the "
"orange qubits represent the 27 mediator qubits.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/docs/images/tutorials/probabilistic-"
"error-cancellation-with-logical-noise-models/extracted-"
"outputs/3675348f-0.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Specify the entangling layers in the circuit with ``samplomatic``. "
"{#specify-the-entangling-layers-in-the-circuit-with-samplomatic}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The Samplomatic ``generate_boxing_pass_manager`` pass groups the circuit's "
"entangling layers into annotated boxes for Pauli twirling and PEC noise "
"injection. The resulting template circuit and samplex (a parametric "
"distribution over the template circuit specifying its twirls and noise "
"injection) are used to define and execute all of the noise learning and "
"sampling QPU experiments. The measurement boxes also carry a ``ChangeBasis``"
" annotation, so the rotations that measure the data qubits in the X basis "
"are supplied to the samplex as an input at sampling time rather than being "
"baked into the circuit.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Below, the same boxing pass is applied to a tiny version of the error-"
"detecting Ising circuit so we can visualize the structure of a boxed "
"circuit.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/docs/images/tutorials/probabilistic-"
"error-cancellation-with-logical-noise-models/extracted-"
"outputs/28dd30dc-0.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Add non-Markovian error checks to the circuit {#add-non-markovian-error-"
"checks-to-the-circuit}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Next, we add [non-Markovian error checks](https://github.com/Qiskit/qiskit-"
"mitigation/blob/main/docs/guides/postselection_with_non_markovian_error_checks.ipynb)"
" to the circuit in order to protect against noise that isn't modeled in our "
"learning protocol. These checks work by implementing a long-pulse bit-flip "
"and ensuring the qubit correctly moved from one classical state to another. "
"If both qubits on an edge fail the check, the sample is discarded. Non-"
"Markovian error checks can be used at the beginning or end of the circuit "
"(or both); here we use them only at the end. We also place checks on unused "
"\"spectator\" qubits adjacent to the circuit, providing more coverage "
"against the noise these checks are designed to detect.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Remember, similar to symmetry checks, this is also a form of postselection, "
"so we need to ensure we are accounting for the drop in postselection rate "
"when combining these techniques. Concretely, if $P(\\text{non-Markovian "
"error}) = \\alpha$ and $P(\\text{symmetry error}) = \\beta$, then you need "
"approximately $\\frac{1}{(1-\\alpha)(1-\\beta)}$ noisy samples to recover "
"one logical sample.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/docs/images/tutorials/probabilistic-"
"error-cancellation-with-logical-noise-models/extracted-"
"outputs/686235cc-0.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Learn the gate and readout noise for the 49-qubit checked-Ising circuit "
"{#learn-the-gate-and-readout-noise-for-the-49-qubit-checked-ising-circuit}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"To mitigate the noise, we must model how the noise is affecting our "
"entangling gates. Here we learn a Pauli-Lindblad noise model for each of the"
" circuit's three unique entangling layers using [qiskit-noise-"
"learning](https://github.com/Qiskit/qiskit-noise-learning). Later, we will "
"prune this noise model of error generators that are detectable by the "
"symmetry checks and mitigate only the remaining noise channel with PEC. The "
"map below shows the three learned layers on the QPU; each qubit is a wheel "
"of its weight-1 X/Y/Z rates, and each coupler a 3×3 grid of the weight-2 "
"Pauli rates on that pair. The $\\gamma$ value of $10$ implies a sampling "
"overhead of $\\gamma^2=10^2=100$.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The readout (TREX) corrections come directly from the SPAM paths of the "
"noise-learning fit. The stem plot below the QPU noise map shows the per-"
"observable TREX rescale factors.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/docs/images/tutorials/probabilistic-"
"error-cancellation-with-logical-noise-models/extracted-"
"outputs/d6b90573-1.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/docs/images/tutorials/probabilistic-"
"error-cancellation-with-logical-noise-models/extracted-"
"outputs/d6b90573-2.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Prune the noise model of terms detectable by the symmetry checks {#prune-"
"the-noise-model-of-terms-detectable-by-the-symmetry-checks}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Each symmetry check is capable of detecting some subset of error generators "
"in the noise model, which means that you don't need to mitigate those "
"errors. Here we create a mask over the detectable noise generators using the"
" ``create_postselected_noise_mask`` function from ``qiskit_mitigation``. "
"This mask will be used to prune the noise model of detectable terms before "
"performing PEC. Performing PEC with respect to the pruned noise model can "
"yield converged expectation values for a fraction of the sampling cost "
"required to perform PEC on the full noise model, as illustrated in the noise"
" model maps above.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The map below is drawn on the same color scale as the learned model above, "
"keeping only the error generators the checks cannot detect. We see that a "
"large number of error generators have been removed from the model, and the "
"sampling overhead has dropped significantly. The sampling overhead to run "
"PEC on the pruned noise model is $\\gamma^2=2.5^2\\approx6.3$, roughly a 16x"
" reduction from the overhead required to mitigate the full noise channel.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/docs/images/tutorials/probabilistic-"
"error-cancellation-with-logical-noise-models/extracted-"
"outputs/758392e9-1.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Sample the error-detecting circuit {#sample-the-error-detecting-circuit}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Here we sample the 49-qubit error-detecting Ising circuit. We enable Pauli "
"twirling and non-Markovian error checks, but we do not perform PEC sampling."
" We will use these samples to calculate baseline expectation values as well "
"as error-detection-only expectation values.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Sample the error-detecting circuit with PEC {#sample-the-error-detecting-"
"circuit-with-pec}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Now we sample again and include PEC randomizations to mitigate the noise the"
" symmetry checks can't detect. These samples will be used to calculate "
"expectation values by using error detection in combination with PEC. Below "
"we plot the accepted shots per PEC randomization against a binomial at the "
"mean acceptance rate. We see that acceptance is uncorrelated with the "
"sampled circuit instance because QED+PEC injects only undetectable Paulis, "
"which commute with the measurements of every mediator qubit.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Collect the samples and verify that PEC randomizations commute with the "
"measurements on the mediator qubits and do not affect the postselection "
"statistics.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/docs/images/tutorials/probabilistic-"
"error-cancellation-with-logical-noise-models/extracted-"
"outputs/5317dcaf-0.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Calculate expectation values and compare strategies {#calculate-expectation-"
"values-and-compare-strategies}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Finally, we calculate all of the expectation values with the "
"``executor_expectation_values`` helper function from ``qiskit-mitigation``, "
"which applies the measurement flips, the postselection masks, the TREX "
"rescale factors, and the quasi-probability signs for us.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**Top chart:** We see that both PEC variants converge, but QED+PEC reaches "
"the $\\pm 0.025$ band with fewer randomizations and tighter error bars. The "
"residual bias in the QED+PEC calculation is below 0.01 and can be attributed"
" to some combination of noise model disagreement, qubit drift over time, and"
" effects from unmodeled noise sources.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**Bottom chart**: The running estimate of the site-averaged $\\langle X "
"\\rangle$ as randomizations accumulate using identical shots per "
"randomization for both PEC variants. PEC + error detection settles into the "
"band within a few thousand randomizations; PEC-only, paying the full "
"sampling overhead, requires many more randomizations to converge.\n"
msgstr ""

